Greek law says nothing about hitchhiking (''οτοστόπ''), so on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://www.e-nomothesia.gr/kat-aytokinita/n-5209-2025.html Article 33(1) of the Road Traffic Code] — law 5209/2025, ΦΕΚ Α΄ 100 of 13 June 2025, which replaced the 1999 code — forbids pedestrians on ''αυτοκινητόδρομοι'' and ''οδοί ταχείας κυκλοφορίας'' '''and on the entry and exit roads to them''', so the slip road is as much out of bounds as the carriageway and the hard shoulder. The same article bans U-turns and reversing there, so a driver who has passed you cannot come back. That leaves the service areas, and the last junction or petrol station before the motorway, as the places to ask for a ride. Everywhere else article 42 simply requires pedestrians to use the pavement or the shoulder, and thumbing from there is unregulated.
